# Kconfig entry point for the Beken SoC family.
# ============================================================================
#
# How HWMv2 assembles this (checked entry by entry against a real Zephyr 4.4.2
# tree and run there, 2026-09-17):
#
# cmake/modules/hwm_v2.cmake runs scripts/list_hardware.py over every SOC_ROOT
# and, for the directory holding this file (the vendor root), generates
#
#       osource "<this directory>/Kconfig"
#       osource "<this directory>/Kconfig.defconfig"
#       osource "<this directory>/Kconfig.soc"
#       osource "<this directory>/Kconfig.sysbuild"
#
# In other words Zephyr only sources those four files directly, and only from
# the vendor root. Files in sub-directories (series / soc) have to be pulled in
# from one of the four with rsource or they never take effect. This is the
# easiest trap to fall into and it reports nothing at all (bk7258's
# Kconfig.defconfig was completely inert because of it).
#
# Same shape upstream, see soc/focaltech, soc/nordic, soc/silabs:
#      Kconfig            <- family symbols + rsource "*/Kconfig"
#      Kconfig.soc        <- rsource "*/Kconfig.soc"
#      Kconfig.defconfig  <- rsource "*/Kconfig.defconfig"
#
# rsource supports globs (scripts/kconfig/kconfiglib.py:398), so "*/Kconfig"
# picks up the same-named file in every series sub-directory.
# ============================================================================

# SoC family symbol, selected in reverse by the series (bk7258).
# Family-wide configuration, if it is ever needed, goes under
# if SOC_FAMILY_BEKEN_ARM.
# Dependency declarations from each series (select only, no type or prompt).
#
# This is deliberately not wrapped in "if SOC_FAMILY_BEKEN_ARM": the series
# Kconfig.soc selects the family, so wrapping the series' selects in the family
# condition makes the two symbols depend on each other and kconfig reports a
# dependency loop.
rsource "*/Kconfig"
